Before any line of the discussion is written, plan it. Decide how the whole discussion goes and what each participant does, consistently with the rules, the schedule and the participants above, and write the plan as one JSON object with exactly these keys:

- "periods": a list with one object per round, in order (Round 1 to Round 3). Each object has:
  - "period": the number;
  - "public": one to three sentences on how the talk goes: the topics raised, the criteria and concerns weighed, what kind of information is shared, how views move in general terms. Describe the *process* only: no outcome and no preference for a named target. Do not name any candidate (neither its name nor its label), nor say which one is favoured or rejected; do not state the vote result, who is executed or what is adopted, or who wins (those go in the fields below);
  - "votes" (Round 3 only): an object mapping every participant to the label of the candidate they vote for;
  - "adopted" (Round 3 only): the label with the most votes.
  - "turns": a list with one object per turn of the talk, in order: {"turn": t, "lines": {participant: the gist of their line in one short sentence, or exactly "Skip" or "Over"}}. Only living participants have lines; a participant who said "Over" has no later line in that round; at most 4 turns.
- "seats": an object with one entry per participant (Jonathan, Shizue, Shion, Kenji, Midori): one or two sentences on what they care about, how they argue and how their view changes over the discussion. Same rule as "public": no candidate name or label, no preference for a named target, no vote result.
- "final": {"adopted": the label with the most votes}.

Write the plan as an observer would describe the discussion: say what is argued and decided, not the wording of the lines. Do not write the discussion itself. Output only the JSON object.

